You can use recursion to draw a pyramid by thinking of a pyramid of height `n` as a pyramid of height `n-1` plus one more row [1]. The base case for the recursion is when the height is 0 or less, at which point the function simply returns without drawing anything [1].

Here's how you can implement this in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void draw(int n)
{
    // Base case: if nothing to draw, return
    if (n <= 0)
    {
        return;
    }

    // Draw pyramid of height n-1
    draw(n - 1);

    // Draw one more row of width n
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("#");
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void)
{
    int height = 4; // Example height
    draw(height);
    return 0;
}
```

In this code:
- The `draw` function takes an integer `n` representing the height of the pyramid to be drawn [1].
- The base case `if (n <= 0)` stops the recursion when there's nothing to draw [1].
- `draw(n - 1)` recursively calls the `draw` function to draw a pyramid of height `n-1` [1].
- The loop `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` prints a row of `n` hashes [1].
- `printf("\n")` moves the cursor to the next line after printing each row [1].

Another example of creating a Mario pyramid using recursion is shown in the notes [4].

Keep in mind that recursion involves a function calling itself, and it's crucial to have a base case to prevent infinite loops [1, 4]. Each recursive call should work on a smaller subproblem, bringing you closer to the base case [4].

